<phyphox version="1.14" locale="en">
  <title>Probe 3: the FFT and buffer slicing work</title>
  <category>Probes</category>
  <description>
        Diagnostic. Takes ten seconds and tells you one thing: whether the Fourier transform, ramps and subranges behave

        Run the probes in order. The FIRST one that fails to import, or shows nothing, names the feature your phyphox version will not do - which turns "none of it works and I have no idea why" into a single specific problem.
    </description>

  <data-containers>
    <container size="256">raw</container>
    <container>n</container>
    <container>nHalf</container>
    <container>nHalf1</container>
    <container size="256">re</container>
    <container size="256">im</container>
    <container size="129">freq</container>
    <container size="128">halfF</container>
    <container size="128">halfRe</container>
    <container size="128">halfIm</container>
    <container size="128">mag</container>
    <container>probe</container>
    <container>level</container>
  </data-containers>

  <input>
    <sensor type="accelerometer" rate="0">
      <output component="abs">raw</output>
    </sensor>
  </input>

  <views>
    <view label="Result">
      <value label="Wobble strength" size="3" precision="1">
        <input>level</input>
      </value>
      <info label="Shake the phone and THIS number should shoot up - from a few units at rest to hundreds or thousands. That is the test. If it climbs when you shake and falls when you stop, the FFT works and so does everything built on it."/>
      <separator height="0.5"/>
      <value label="Frequency of the strongest wobble" precision="2">
        <input>probe</input>
      </value>
      <info label="Do not read too much into this one. Shaking by hand is one to three times a second, which is below the lowest frequency this can resolve, so it pins to the bottom of the scale while you shake and drifts upwards when you stop and it is left picking out noise. That is correct behaviour, not a fault - the real experiments all ignore the lowest bins for exactly this reason."/>
      <graph label="Spectrum" labelX="Bin" labelY="Level">
        <input axis="x">halfF</input>
        <input axis="y">mag</input>
      </graph>
    </view>
  </views>
  <analysis sleep="0.5">
    <count>
      <input clear="false">raw</input>
      <output clear="false">n</output>
    </count>
    <fft>
      <input clear="false">raw</input>
      <output as="re">re</output>
      <output as="im">im</output>
    </fft>
    <divide>
      <input clear="false">n</input>
      <input type="value">2</input>
      <output clear="false">nHalf</output>
    </divide>
    <add>
      <input clear="false">nHalf</input>
      <input type="value">1</input>
      <output>nHalf1</output>
    </add>
    <ramp>
      <input as="start" type="value">0</input>
      <input as="stop" type="value">50</input>
      <input as="length" clear="false">nHalf1</input>
      <output>freq</output>
    </ramp>
    <subrange>
      <input as="from" type="value">1</input>
      <input as="to" clear="false">nHalf</input>
      <input as="in">freq</input>
      <input as="in">re</input>
      <input as="in">im</input>
      <output>halfF</output>
      <output>halfRe</output>
      <output>halfIm</output>
    </subrange>
    <formula formula="sqrt([1_]*[1_]+[2_]*[2_])">
      <input>halfRe</input>
      <input>halfIm</input>
      <output>mag</output>
    </formula>
    <max>
      <input as="x" clear="false">halfF</input>
      <input as="y" clear="false">mag</input>
      <output as="position">probe</output>
      <output as="max">level</output>
    </max>
  </analysis>

  <export>
    <set name="Probe">
      <data name="value">probe</data>
    </set>
  </export>
</phyphox>
